From: rjc@gnu.ai.mit.edu (Ray)
Date: Mon, 11 Jul 94 11:43:14 WET DST
Subject: [#94-7-86] emergence defined

nancy@genie.slhs.udel.edu writes:
> 
> "Peter C. McCluskey" <pcm@world.std.com> writes:
> 
> > At Alife 4, Vince Darley (vince@das.harvard.edu) proposed this definition:
> >"A true emergent phenomenon is one for which the optimal means of prediction
> >is simulation".
> 
> Cool--and this leads to a nice parallel between emergent behavior and
> random sequences.
> 
> How certain is it that quantum behavior is random rather than chaotic?

   Chaotic processes are locally determinstic (i.e. measure them
quick enough and you can predict a very short interval ahead)

   Quantum mechanics is such that the outcome of any one experiment
can not be determined, no matter how much "history" you know about the
experiment. I'm pretty sure this is a feature of the mathematical model
itself. i.e. QM says nothing about what the outcome of an experiment
will be, only what the probable outcomes *might be*

  I'm not at all sure that QM is *proof* of nondeterminism. I don't
think it can logically rule out an underlying determinism. Mike
Price probably knows more.


