From: derek@cs.wisc.edu (Derek Zahn)
Date: Mon, 11 Jul 1994 13:12:28 -0500 (CDT)
Subject: [#94-7-88] emergence defined

Peter McCluskey:

>  At Alife 4, Vince Darley (vince@das.harvard.edu) proposed this definition:
> "A true emergent phenomenon is one for which the optimal means of prediction
> is simulation".

This seems hopelessly confused to me.  

  * solving analytically for the positions of the balls on my
    frictionless Platonian Pool Table is pretty intractable after
    a while... much easier to simulate it.  I see nothing particularly
    emergent there.
  
  * what is meant by "optimal" (that is, by what measurement --
    computer cycles on a VAX, man-hours, or what?  And why?)
    Also, since we can never tell what's optimal for sure, this
    definition is useless in actually identifying emergent
    phenomena.
  
  * what is "simulation"?  When we write computer programs or
    mathematical expressions we are building formal models
    of the phenomena under study.  We make predictions by
    manipulating those representations according to the rules
    of the model.  How are we to decide which modeling techniques
    are simulations and which aren't?
  
  * is "simulation" really the "optimal" way to predict, say,
    the migrating patterns of birds?  Many emergent phenomena
    are chaotic in nature, making their prediction highly
    dependent on very small changes in initial conditions.
    Given that perfect measurement of those conditions is
    impossible, how could a prediction given by a simulation
    be optimal?

derek


