From: doug@OpenMind.com (Doug Cutrell)
Date: Mon, 11 Jul 1994 12:52:58 -0800
Subject: [#94-7-90] emergence defined

Peter McCluskey writes:

>  At Alife 4, Vince Darley (vince@das.harvard.edu) proposed this definition:
> "A true emergent phenomenon is one for which the optimal means of prediction
> is simulation".

A while back, in message number 94-6-122, Derek Zahn wrote:

>For example, we certainly
>would like to say that:
>
> memetics and politics are emergent phenomena
> speciation is an emergent phenomenon
> higher mathematics shows emergent order
> and so on.

These test cases seem to rule Vince Darley's proposed definition as
inadequate.  I think this is natural... the mistake is in trying to come up
with a single "definition" that will apply in all the cases we would like
it to.  There are different domains of discourse, and the term "emergence"
serves somewhat different functions in each.  Darley's definition probably
serves admirably within the limited domain of neural networks, genetic
algorithms, and similar topics.  It is probably quite useful in these
domains, and I doubt that any definition which would also serve for
"memetics" could be substituted in these cases, without losing something.

Perhaps there is something analogous occurring in these other domains:  for
example, maybe we can always find two or more different levels of
explanation or "model", with an asymmetric embedding of one inside the
other (memetics and politics embedded within the attitudes and behavior of
all the individuals of a population, etc.)  But I would hold that no matter
what kind of definition you come up with, there will always be cases in
which it does not seem to really apply.  For example, I don't think that
"higher mathematics" fits into my just described definition.

This does not mean that the search is useless -- but I'd suggest the focus
be inclusive, seeking to collect a body of definitions that collectively
serve our purposes, rather than exclusive, seeking to find the single
universal definition.  With this in mind, I think the relevant question
with regards to Vince Darley's definition is "What are the different
domains where this definition works?"  I submit that it does work for
neural networks, cellular automata, and genetic algorithms.  Are there any
other domains in which it also works?

Doug


