From: price@price.demon.co.uk (Michael Clive Price)
Date: Mon, 11 Jul 94 16:39:21 GMT
Subject: [#94-7-95] emergence defined

Peter C. McCluskey:
>  At Alife 4, Vince Darley (vince@das.harvard.edu) proposed this 
> definition:
> "A true emergent phenomenon is one for which the optimal means 
>  of prediction is simulation".

Nancy Lebovitz:
> Cool--

Yup, pretty neat.

> and this leads to a nice parallel between emergent behavior and
> random sequences.

Pseudo-random, IMO.

> How certain is it that quantum behavior is random rather than chaotic?

Very certain, 'though it's kinda hard to explain.  There's just been a
"lively" thread on sci.physics on this.  It is rather unlikely that
there is any connection between chaos and quantum-randomness.

The debate was about whether radioactive decay was "truly" random or
whether there could be hidden differences between one nuclei/ particle/
etc and another that are responsible for the time of decay.  The
solution relies on noting that the exclusion principle (the thing that
stops electrons around an atom all dropping into the lowest orbital)
implies that there are no hidden differences between same-type
particles.  That all same-type particles (eg all electrons or all
neutrons) are identical in principle as well as in appearance.  If there
was extra hidden machinery inside particles then this effect - and
quantum interference in general - would not work.

Chaos implies that very slight differences between states are magnified
with time.  But quantum-interference says there is no difference to
begin with.

