From: derek@cs.wisc.edu (Derek Zahn)
Date: Mon, 11 Jul 1994 19:48:08 -0500 (CDT)
Subject: [#94-7-96] AI development acceleration

Eric Watt Forste:

> > those who see AI as potentially arising emergently from a much more
> > undifferentiated complex system.
> 
> > those who see AI as something that can be evolved or otherwise
> > searched for. Such search becomes cheaper and cheaper.
> 
> How do you distinguish between these two? I must be missing something,
> because, generalized, they seem to me to be mere restatements of one
> another.

The "undifferentiated complex system" (actually an oxymoron as no
undifferentiated system is complex) has certain characteristics:
e.g. a learning rule, the "physics" of interaction, etc.  The
question is:  What specific characteristics lead to the desired
result, and how do we find them?

This "third" view is fairly agnostic about the extent to which
the AI system possesses complex structure prior to pressing its
on button.  Clearly, either designing a monstrously complex
structure or finding a suitable substrate is a very difficult
task, but both are castable in terms of searching through
alternatives instead of by applying brainpower.

derek


